  "code": "1675",
  "cost": 0,
  "interpretation": "<ul>\n<li style=\"font-size: 9px;\" data-start=\"939\" data-end=\"1099\"><span style=\"font-size: 9px;\"><strong data-start=\"939\" data-end=\"951\">Positive</strong>: Suggests presence of <strong data-start=\"974\" data-end=\"1002\">amyloid protein deposits</strong> in tissue; seen in conditions like <strong data-start=\"1038\" data-end=\"1098\">AL amyloidosis, AA amyloidosis, and familial amyloidosis</strong>.</span></li>\n<li style=\"font-size: 9px;\" data-start=\"1100\" data-end=\"1170\">\n<p data-start=\"1102\" data-end=\"1170\"><span style=\"font-size: 9px;\"><strong data-start=\"1102\" data-end=\"1114\">Negative</strong>: No amyloid deposition detected in the examined tissue.</span></p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><span style=\"font-size: 9px;\">Congo Red stain is highly specific for amyloid. Confirmation with polarization microscopy and correlation with <strong data-start=\"1285\" data-end=\"1356\">clinical history, serum protein electrophoresis, and immunofixation</strong> is often necessary</span></p>",
